R/qtl users:  I have been running text batchfiles of R/qtl commands overnight on my laptop, but in some cases of extensive text output (lists mainly), the early part of the R/qtl R batchfile output text is not retained (apparently it has scrolled off the end of the top of the record of console text).  Is there any way to increase the amount of text output retained in the R console so that I can recover it in the morning?  - Jim

From u963b6d84a823404f at groups.google.com  Fri Nov 16 20:59:24 2012
From: u963b6d84a823404f at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Fri, 16 Nov 2012 20:59:24 +0000
Subject: Re: [Rqtl-disc] Retaining Rqtl text output
Message-ID: <hSnHHquVE3I.XQFDZlFynAcJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <hSnHHquVE3I.RzK8BdbPiz8J@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <hSnHHquVE3I.RzK8BdbPiz8J@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/hSnHHquVE3I/m/XQFDZlFynAcJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It would be best to run R from the command line, using R CMD BATCH (see http://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-patched/library/utils/html/BATCH.html). For example:

 R CMD BATCH my_input.R the_output.txt

Then the_output.txt should contain absolutely everything.

Also, I would generally save the important results to files, using code like

 save(object, file="object.RData")

karl

